WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:05.310
Stranger Things 3 is finally here, along with
the Red Army, parasitic sludge monsters, and

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.260
more '80s references than you can shake an
ice cream scooper at.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.300
Of course, '80s nostalgia isn't all you'll
find if you dig deeper.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:15.320
Here are just a few of the small details you
might've missed in Stranger Things 3.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.860
One of the earliest pop culture references
in Stranger Things 3 comes with Dustin's return

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:22.200
to Hawkins from summer camp.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.500
Dustin tells everyone about his new girlfriend
Suzie, explaining that the only way to contact

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:30.200
her is his newly constructed ham radio, which
he nicknames "Cerebro."

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:32.999
"So it's a ham radio."

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.260
"The Cadillac of ham radios."

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:38.850
Anyone familiar with Marvel's X-Men will know
where Dustin gets the name.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:43.600
In comics and movies and everything in between,
Cerebro is the device Professor X uses to

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.020
amplify his telepathic powers to find other
super-powered mutants.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:51.260
With her psychic abilities, Eleven is the
character in Stranger Things most like Professor

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:54.800
X. Ironically she's one of the few of the
series' kids who isn't there for Cerebro's

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:55.300
unveiling.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:59.300
Dustin gets everyone to agree to hike with
him to Hawkins' tallest hill so they can set

00:00:59.309 --> 00:01:04.010
up Cerebro and contact Suzie, but Eleven and
Mike bail before they finish the climb, presumably

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:05.740
to find a good spot to make out.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.140
Dustin's Cerebro ends up being a pop culture
reference standing on top of another pop culture

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:11.640
reference.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:15.280
He doesn't use the name earlier when he leads
his friends there, but in the season finale

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:18.340
we learn Dustin has given a name to the hill
where he keeps Cerebro - Weathertop.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.159
He says the name when Steve takes him and
Erica to Cerebro, speaking it as if Steve

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:24.680
should just naturally know what he means.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:29.020
If your memory isn't that great or you're
not a Tolkien fan, Weathertop is a ruin atop

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:30.580
a hill in J.R.R.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:31.719
Tolkien's Fellowship of the Ring.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:35.500
Shortly after they meet one another, Aragorn
and the hobbits camp there on their way to

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:36.780
the Elven land of Rivendell.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:40.759
It's where the heroes are briefly surrounded
by the RingWraiths, and where Frodo is stabbed

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:44.580
in the arm by the Witch King of Angmor and
corrupted by the undead warrior's blade.

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:49.360
As not only a self-proclaimed nerd but a Dungeons
&amp; Dragons player, Dustin is definitely familiar

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.880
with the Lord of the Rings books, hence the
name he gives the hill.

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:56.999
And even if he wasn't big on fantasy novels,
he could certainly be a fan of the 1978 animated

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.060
Lord of the Rings film.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:03.300
In the beginning of Stranger Things 3's fourth
episode, "The Sauna Test," Max offers Eleven

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:07.240
one of two comic books to read, and those
specific issues seem to have been chosen for

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:08.000
a few reasons.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:10.080
Most obviously, they're accurate for the time.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:15.980
Wonder Woman #326 was released in 1985 - the
year in which Stranger Things 3 was set - while

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:19.660
Green Lantern #185 came out in 1984.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:23.560
Both comics end with the reveal of a villain
reminiscent of the Mind Flayer.

00:02:23.569 --> 00:02:28.850
In Green Lantern #185, test pilot Richard
Davis unwillingly flies a jet into a giant

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:33.510
black column and inside he finds Eclipso - a
mystically parasitic supervillain known for

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:36.500
controlling and corrupting hosts, just like
the Mind Flayer.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:42.080
Likewise, Wonder Woman #326 ends with the
emergence of an Aztec god who's described

00:02:42.099 --> 00:02:46.840
as, among other things, "the god of the nocturnal
sky… the embodiment of change through conflict."

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:51.420
Both concepts are powerfully present in Stranger
Things 3, particularly the concept of change.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:56.590
Stranger Things has always worn its cinematic
inspirations as badges of honor, and one particular

00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:59.520
moment in season 3 proves that idea pretty
clearly.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:03.239
When the Scoop Troop leaves the movie theater
while trying to escape the Russians, we see

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:09.580
a bunch of 1985 movies on the marquee, including
The Stuff, Return to Oz, DARYL, Fletch, and

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:10.100
Cocoon.

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:11.280
What happens in The Stuff?

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:15.740
Residents of a small town all eat a ton of
an addictive food-sludge called, well, "the

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:16.240
Stuff."

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:20.760
Turns out that the stuff is actually a parasitic
goop that takes over people's brains and turns

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.500
them into mindless zombies, before eventually
eating them.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:25.060
"Just like the movie."

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:29.580
As for the other movies, Return to Oz includes
a batch of bad guys called "Wheelers," a name

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:31.560
they share with Nancy and Mike Wheeler.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:36.019
DARYL is about a secret government science
experiment to create a child-like super-soldier

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:38.050
for the military, just like Eleven's training.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:41.960
Fletch features a journalist who investigates
a bigger story after his editor tells him

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:46.100
not to, similar to Nancy's pursuit of the
story she hears from Mrs. Driscoll.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:49.140
And Cocoon is about a bunch of aliens on Earth
disguised as humans.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:50.380
Sound familiar?

